WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:15.620
The Vehicle System Testbed is an engineering model version of the Perseverance Rover, which

00:15.620 --> 00:19.300
means that it has all the same electronics as Perseverance.

00:19.300 --> 00:23.280
It's important for Perseverance to have a twin so that we have a place where we can

00:23.280 --> 00:26.880
test things before we actually operate Perseverance.

00:27.840 --> 00:34.080
Then once we do land on Mars, we need the twin to test out all of the sequences and plans that we

00:34.080 --> 00:39.040
have for the Rover before we actually send them up to Perseverance on the surface of Mars.

00:39.040 --> 00:44.720
Also, if we have a problem with Perseverance, the VSTB is going to give us a place to practice

00:44.720 --> 00:46.160
how we recover from that problem.

00:46.480 --> 00:56.960
This is a really big milestone for us today. This is the first time we've ever driven a testbed.

00:58.400 --> 01:05.360
So by driving, we're validating that mechanically. We can drive the Rover on its own weight, on Earth.

01:06.160 --> 01:07.920
The VSTB is now ready to drive on the Mars.

01:07.920 --> 01:18.240
Today, we're moving the VSTB from its assembly facility to the Mars here.

01:38.080 --> 01:46.240
I'm extremely proud of what the VSTB team has accomplished today. I'm really excited to see

01:46.240 --> 01:50.240
this VSTB out in the Mars yard where we can truly test mobility for the first time.

02:01.280 --> 02:05.120
Today is a great day for the testbed. It's the first time that we've driven optimism out into

02:05.120 --> 02:19.840
the Mars yard. We get to actually driving the dirt and get this thing dirty and do some mobility testing.

